welcome back to cbs saturday morning we  begin this half hour with the final  preparations for the state funeral of  queen elizabeth ii  king charles iii will host president  biden along with 500 other heads of  state and foreign dignitaries at  buckingham palace tomorrow on friday the  king and his siblings including princess  anne held a silent vigil around their  mother's coffin in westminster hall  today the queen's eight grandchildren  will do the same  overnight mourners near windsor castle  witnessed a rehearsal of the queen's  burial process the funeral has been  planned for decades with details  especially important to the queen  roxanna saberi has more from london  the last century has witnessed powerful  moments of public grief from the  assassination of a president to the  passing of an icon and the death of a  princess  but on monday in london a nation and the  world will say a final farewell to the  woman who outlived them all at queen  elizabeth's state funeral how much  rehearsing is going into it the queen's  funeral has been rehearsed almost  annually every year and  for many many years  britain's last state funeral was in 1965  for winston churchill who led the  country through world war ii winston  churchill was much loved by the british  public but  the queen is in a different stratosphere  to winston churchill and i think  her state funeral will reflect that  troops from commonwealth countries will  take part in a procession being  rehearsed around the clock that former  bbc royal correspondent wesley kerr  predicts will draw at least a million  people to the streets everything has  been done to enable the largest number  of people and there seems to be an  insatiable demand  to see her late majesty's coffin but in  a very dignified way  some elements of a state funeral follow  tradition members of the royal navy will  tow the queen's coffin on a gun carriage  a ritual that began in 1901 when horses  pulling queen victoria's funeral  carriage panicked and sailors stepped in  but we'll also see personal touches  choreographed by queen elizabeth herself  in this covid traumatized world doctors  and nurses will march on monday and the  queen's strong faith will resonate  throughout they'll be her favorite hymns  there'll be particular prayers  come in to honor a monarch who met 13  u.s presidents and visited over 100  countries around 500 heads of state and  foreign dignitaries are expected putting  diplomacy front and center expecting it  to go off smoothly our government is  pretty good at figuring out who can sit  next to who and who can't  britain's security services have to  figure out how to keep them safe this is  the biggest policing operation uk  policing has ever undertaken the weight  of responsibility now rests with the  metropolitan police service  the day will start at westminster hall  but will end quietly in windsor at st  george's chapel where the queen will be  laid to rest with the man she could no  longer live without  for cbs saturday morning roxanna seberry  london  stay with us and stay with cbs news for  full coverage of queen elizabeth's  funeral on monday morning our live  coverage begins at 5 30 eastern right  here on cbs and on the cbs news  streaming network
 

